ely after the Landlord, my role is to "block" (Ding) effectively. The Landlord played a Single 7. If I play a middle-range card like an 8, 9, or J, I risk allowing the Landlord to beat it with a card like a 10, Q, or K, thereby reducing their hand to 1 card and gaining the initiative to play their final card.\n\nReason: To prevent the Landlord from coasting to victory, I must play a card high enough to force out a 2 (the highest remaining rank, as Jokers are gone) or force a pass. The Single Ace is the best choice for this. Although I have a pair of Aces, breaking it is necessary to control the Landlord. If the Landlord has a 2, they will be forced to play it now; if they don\'t, I gain control of the turn.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♠A"]}'}
